  "response": "## Verdict: request changes — don't block\n\nThe helper is `src/date.ts:51-52`:\n\n```ts\nexport function tomorrowMs(nowMs: number): number {\n  return nowMs + 86400000;\n}\n```\n\n`crimes` charges it as **DST-Naive Day Math** (`crime_00018`, `dst_naive_arithmetic`). Its evidence, verbatim:\n\n- `\"+ 86400000 (day) @L52\"`\n- `\"lines: 52\"`\n- `\"use a calendar-aware library (Luxon, Temporal, date-fns-tz) for day math\"`\n\nand its summary: `\"1 day-level millisecond constant in arithmetic. A day isn't 86,400,000 ms on DST-transition days; manual ms math drifts silently.\"`\n\n**Why request-changes and not block.** The finding is `\"severity\": \"medium\"` / `\"confidence\": 0.8`, with `\"blast_radius\": 0` and `\"blast_radius_direct_importers\": 0` — and grepping the fixture confirms it: `tomorrowMs` has no callers yet, and `src/date.ts` has no test file. So nothing today can be wrong because of it; the defect is latent. Combined with `\"effort\": \"medium\"` and `\"fix_shape\": \"use a DST-aware library; never add seconds to a wall-clock date\"`, this is a fix worth landing before a caller appears, not an incident.\n\n**What would flip it to block:** if this feeds an entitlement or money decision. Same file, `crime_00001` (`direct_date`, severity high, `agent_risk` 0.44) records `\"1 decide a branch or comparison (lines 4)\"` — that's `isExpired`. The moment `tomorrowMs(...)` output flows into `isExpired`, an hour of DST drift becomes a grant/deny on a trial or billing period, and I'd hold the PR. Ask the author what the first caller will be.\n\n**Two related findings on the same file, worth folding into the same fix:** `crime_00009` (Half-UTC, Half-Local — `\"\\\"d\\\" uses getUTCFullYear() @L9 and getMonth() @L41\"`) and `crime_00011` (Timezone Roulette — `\"unsafe literals: \\\"2026-12-25T07:00:00\\\", \\\"2026-12-20\\\"\"`). Adopting Temporal or Luxon for `tomorrowMs` fixes all three time-domain issues at once.\n\n## The two wrong dates per year\n\nAdding 86,400,000 ms is *exactly correct* as an instant offset — UTC has no DST. It's wrong the moment a caller reads the result as \"the same wall-clock time on the next calendar day.\" That breaks on the two DST transitions per year in the caller's zone:\n\n| Zone family | 2026 spring-forward (day is 23h) | 2026 fall-back (day is 25h) |\n|---|---|---|\n| US / Canada (`America/*`) | **Sun 8 March 2026** | **Sun 1 November 2026** |\n| EU / UK (`Europe/*`) | **Sun 29 March 2026** | **Sun 25 October 2026** |\n\nConcretely, on `America/New_York`:\n\n- **8 March 2026** — 02:00 local jumps to 03:00. `tomorrowMs` called at 09:00 on 7 March returns an instant that reads **10:00 on 8 March**: one hour late.\n- **1 November 2026** — 02:00 local repeats back to 01:00. Called at 09:00 on 31 October, it returns **08:00 on 1 November**: one hour early.\n\nThe sharper failure is the calendar day itself, since the drift can push the result across midnight:\n\n- Called at **23:30 on 7 March**, +24h lands at **00:30 on 9 March** — `tomorrow` skips a day.\n- Called at **00:30 on 31 October**, +24h lands at **23:30 on 31 October** — `tomorrow` returns *today*.\n\nSo the affected inputs are timestamps in the 24 hours preceding each transition; the two calendar dates a reviewer should put in the test cases are the transition dates above. Zones without DST (`UTC`, `Asia/Tokyo`, most of `Asia/Kolkata`) are unaffected — which is exactly why this survives CI on a UTC runner and fails for users.\n\n## Suggested comment on the PR\n\nTake the finding's own `suggested_actions[0]`: `\"Replace ms-arithmetic with a timezone-aware addDays/subtract operation that knows about DST and leap years.\"` In practice — `Temporal.ZonedDateTime.from({...}).add({days: 1})`, with the zone passed in rather than inferred from the host, plus two unit tests pinned to the transition dates above.\n\n---\n\nOne measurement note: `crimes scan src/date.ts --format json` returned findings for the whole repo (42 findings across 21 files, `src/billing.ts`, `src/api/*`, assets, docs), not just the path argument. I filtered to `src/date.ts` myself. If the path argument is meant to scope the scan, that's a CLI bug worth a separate issue.",
